ue is dropped."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" * If you want to replace the values of two variables, see [`swap`]."},{"DocComment":" * If you want to replace with a default value, see [`take`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" A simple example:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::mem;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let mut v: Vec<i32> = vec![1, 2];"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let old_v = mem::replace(&mut v, vec![3, 4, 5]);"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(vec![1, 2], old_v);"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(vec![3, 4, 5], v);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `replace` allows consumption of a struct field by replacing it with another value."},{"DocComment":" Without `replace` you can run into issues like these:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```compile_fail,E0507"},{"DocComment":" struct Buffer<T> { buf: Vec<T> }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" impl<T> Buffer<T> {"},{"DocComment":"     fn replace_index(&mut self, i: usize, v: T) -> T {"},{"DocComment":"         // error: cannot move out of dereference of `&mut`-pointer"},{"DocComment":"         let t = self.buf[i];"},{"DocComment":"         self.buf[i] = v;"},{"DocComment":"         t"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Note that `T` does not necessarily implement [`Clone`], so we can't even clone `self.buf[i]` to"},{"DocComment":" avoid the move. But `replace` can be used to disassociate the original value at that index from"},{"DocComment":" `self`, allowing it to be returned:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" # #![allow(dead_code)]"},{"DocComment":" use std::mem;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # struct Buffer<T> { buf: Vec<T> }"},{"DocComment":" impl<T> Buffer<T> {"},{"DocComment":"     fn replace_index(&mut self, i: usize, v: T) -> T {"},{"DocComment":"         mem::replace(&mut self.buf[i], v)"},